# INVARIANT CHECK — "default-inert new capability" (the skill's invariant #6), made
# mechanical instead of aspirational.
#
# Surface FX (#27) is documented as "additive translucency/SSS + view-angle
# iridescence, inert at 0". This asserts the inert half: with both FX explicitly at
# zero, the frame must be *identical* to the same look without them — 06-fx-baseline.
# No golden is involved; both frames are captured in this same run, seconds apart.
#
# If this fails, Surface FX is leaking at zero, and every preset that predates it has
# silently changed appearance.
#
# ⚠️ The reference is 06-fx-baseline, NOT 01-original-standard, even though the two
# set identical parameters. Frozen scenes freeze rotation *speed*; the phase
# accumulator (`World::angle` / `wind_phase`) is not reset by `set` or by `release`, so
# two frames of the same scene captured either side of an animating scene sit at
# different rotation phases. Pointing this check at 01 measured that clock drift rather
# than Surface FX and failed on its first real run — see 06-fx-baseline's header for
# the full account. Keep the reference adjacent.
#!desc      Surface FX explicitly at 0 must reproduce the plain Standard look exactly.
#!checks    nonblack
#!expect    same-as 06-fx-baseline
#!tolerance 0.002

generator "organic math"
surface original
material standard

set rot_mod_x 0 rot_mod_y 0 rot_mod_z 0
set rot_amp_x 0 rot_amp_y 0 rot_amp_z 0
set trans_amp_x 0 trans_amp_y 0 trans_amp_z 0
set scale_amp 0
set cam_path 0 cam_speed 0 cam_kick 0

set exposure 0 metallic 0.1 roughness 0.5
set ambient 0.3 glow 0.2 bloom_intensity 0.2

# The assertion: these are the only lines that differ from 06-fx-baseline, and they
# are no-ops. Keep that true — a parameter added here but not there stops this scene
# testing Surface FX and starts it testing the parameter.
set iridescence 0 irid_scale 0 irid_shift 0
set subsurface 0 sss_distortion 0 sss_power 0
